# Dedicated controller drivers

When adding support for a **new** controller in this OGX-Mini 2026 fork:

## Core rule

**Do not modify an existing controller driver to add support for a different controller.**

Create a **new dedicated driver** (host class / Bluepad32 parser / module) by default. Existing working drivers are effectively **read-only** unless the user explicitly instructs otherwise or a shared subsystem change is unavoidable.

**Adding Controller B must not risk breaking Controller A.**

## Required shape

- New detection rules (VID/PID/descriptors) that claim **only** that controller/family
- Own init/handshake, report parse, mappings, rumble/LED/quirks/timing
- Register via `HardwareIDs.h` / `HostManager` / Bluepad32 without changing other drivers’ behavior
- Prefer `HostDriver/MyPad/MyPad.cpp` over growing `if (device == …)` chains inside XboxOne, PS4, SwitchPro, etc.

## Shared code

Reuse genuinely common helpers (base `HostDriver`, USB/HID utilities, PadIn mapping helpers). Do **not** put controller-specific exceptions into a shared or existing driver because two pads look similar.

## Variants

Only share a driver when there is **strong evidence** the hardware is functionally identical (same protocol + report layout). When uncertain, **separate drivers first**; consolidate later after testing.

## Before editing an existing driver

Ask: *Can this live entirely in the new driver?* If yes, do that. If a shared change is required, keep it minimal and preserve existing driver behavior.

## Exception

Modify an existing driver for a new pad **only** when the user explicitly asks, or when there is an unavoidable architectural reason (document it in the PR).
